Based on my search of the software organization, I can confirm that the switch + file_exists pattern is consistently used across LEAF apps as a platform-wide convention. Here's what I found:

Pattern Confirmation
All LEAF report.php files follow the same resolver pattern:

A switch statement handles a few hardcoded action names (e.g., 'about', 'showServiceFTEstatus')
A default case with the fallback: if ($action != '' && file_exists("templates/reports/{$action}.tpl"))
Direct template fetch using the action name: $t_form->fetch("reports/{$action}.tpl")
This pattern appears consistently in:

LEAF/LEAF_Nexus/report.php
LEAF/LEAF_Request_Portal/report.php
LEAF-launchpad/src/launchpad/report.php
The search results are limited, so there may be additional instances, but these samples show uniform implementation.

Regarding Action Aliases
Based on the code I reviewed, the alias approach should be safe with these important caveats:

What's NOT checked:

No database allowlist validation of action names
No permissions table keyed by action name in the routing logic
No hardcoded action validation beyond template file existence
What IS checked:

XSSHelpers::xscrub() sanitizes the $_GET['a'] parameter before use—so path traversal (e.g., ../../../) is blocked
file_exists() must pass—the template file must actually exist
Login/session checks happen before the switch, but not per-action
To safely add an alias:

Create a second .tpl file in templates/reports/ with the alias name
If the original template has per-action permission logic inside it (JavaScript, PHP business logic), it applies equally to both names
The alias will reuse all JavaScript assets and form logic from the included template
No changes needed to routing config, settings, or permissions tables
Potential exceptions to be aware of: If an app implements per-action permission validation inside the template (in inline PHP or via API calls), the alias inherits those checks. Verify whether the template being aliased contains action-specific security guards that might behave unexpectedly when called by a different action name.s
